WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.200
Happy School Choice Week.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.260
Choice is a good thing, but

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.080
in much of America, students don’t much choice.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.080
Kids are assigned schools by zip code,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.040
and then many of those schools teach kids that

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.840
America is terrible and capitalism hurts people.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.540
So I started a charity that offers teachers free videos

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.980
that introduce kids to free market ideas.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.980
It should be in more classrooms,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.520
just to spark more conversation.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.040
These young people watched the videos in high school

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.600
because this teacher downloaded them from our

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.660
website and played them in class.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.780
I want to just show us a brief clip.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:36.760
They were amazing.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.880
They really opened up my mind to think differently.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.300
It changed the way that I viewed the world.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.960
When I originally went to school,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.080
it was all taught from one side.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.820
This country is horrible because you are a minority,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.140
you can’t make it.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.560
It made me dislike the country initially.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.060
Then he watched the videos.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.690
This woman who came here from Greece says America

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.040
doesn’t pressure you to follow a kind of living.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.560
People can express themselves

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.160
and be whatever they want to be.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.300
America is a place where we get to write the script

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.100
of our own lives.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.460
When these videos were shown to me.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.700
I felt ashamed for what I initially believed.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.200
Gabriel then enlisted in the National Guard.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.360
I wanted to give back

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.800
to not only giving my family so much opportunity,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.980
but also to protect, defend, and serve

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.500
the people in United States.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.160
Diony Perez says our videos made him want

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.120
to take responsibility for himself.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.120
We never really thought like this before,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.920
you know, we're taught that the government is -

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.820
They're responsible for us and

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.540
we have to trust them in doing everything for us.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.660
So he started his own business.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.500
Welcome to Familia Motor Group.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.620
I have an auto leasing company.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:45.620
Receive your brand new vehicle at your doorsteps.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.120
I definitely think the Stossel videos helped me

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.320
become more of an entrepreneur.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.640
Just do the work be independent

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.440
don’t expect anyone to do anything for you

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.040
don’t expect any handouts.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.340
Just get it done.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.740
Good, market and - political - great.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.380
Many students had never heard that

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.340
competition and markets can be useful.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.180
I wasn't really exposed to any of those ideas.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.700
I never saw it before.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.040
I never heard about like this side of the story.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.040
800 million people out of poverty in

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.220
South East Asia, because they implemented

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.400
a little bit of capitalism.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.060
If I'm wrong about this what else am I wrong about?

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.360
The videos come with teacher’s guides,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.180
full length classroom lessons with discussion

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.680
questions and writing assignments.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.700
Even if people disagree with the video,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.500
that's kind of good to talk about too.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.820
After a year of watching the videos,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.220
students tell us that certain ideas stuck with them.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.580
One was -

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.920
Unintended consequences the government

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.920
will try to do things to make it

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.230
beneficial to the public.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.120
But it turns out actually made things much worse.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.320
For example:

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.260
He did a piece about minimum wage and how

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.240
increasing it would supposedly be better

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.180
for young working class people,

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:53.520
but it turned out the unintended consequences

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.040
were the minimum wage increase priced out

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.160
young people from getting jobs into the market.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.580
Other students were surprised at how hard it is

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.500
to start a business in America.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.600
Do you have a license?

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.460
Stossel goes over to Hong Kong

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.540
and he gets a license in just two hours.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.030
Thank you, sir.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.540
Rather than over here.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.080
It takes months and even years.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.320
I know the struggle that

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.540
my parents had to go through to open their business.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.840
So that was kind of the turning point for me.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.000
One particular video that really piqued my interest was

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.300
"The Evil Rich."

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.640
The top 1%!

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.460
People really think that the 1% is evil

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.900
because they make so much money.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.420
But the video explains

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.520
the rich could only get rich by inventing better things.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.880
Things we want.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.500
If it wasn't for Steve Jobs, like creating,

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.360
these iPhones or creating like the Mac computers

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.620
or the Mac laptops,

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.320
our life would not be as easy as it is today.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.040
This is one of the most incoherent things

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.440
about the regimes.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.060
For example, this video called "Death by Socialism"

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.020
It contradicts all these beliefs

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.900
that how socialism is great and how it helps like the

00:03:58.900 --> 00:03:59.740
poor and all that.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.380
And it kind of showed us like,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.080
Oh, it's not really that great.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.760
After watching in class,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.800
many students share what they learned.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.080
I went to my mom I was like,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:09.880
"Do you know

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.060
who's really paying for all this government stuff?"

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.420
Every time I talk to my friends then

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.700
we bring up anything about politics.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.060
I bring up this new idea

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.280
that I learned on a Stossel video

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.220
and they'd be like, "Really?

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.260
Like I've never known that."

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.580
10 Million students now discuss these videos in class.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.460
Like he said unintended consequences.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.600
For example government, how they give people welfare.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.680
Why video?

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.160
Isn’t a textbook enough?

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.340
A lot of people, well at least my age group,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.340
they don't like to read and analyze.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.280
They prefer to watch videos.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.640
So now our charity helps explain free markets to

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.040
students who rarely hear about that in schools.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.580
School choice doesn’t only mean where you go to school,

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.840
it also means what you get to learn.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.200
And education choice doesn’t have to end with school.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.760
Even now that I graduated high school,

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.280
I'm not watching Stossel in the Classroom there,

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.960
but I'm watching Stossel at home.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.960
Get out of my life!

